owers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zhuzi-z/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zhuzi-z/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zhuzi-z/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zhuzi-z/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zhuzi-z/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zhuzi-z/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zhuzi-z/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zhuzi-z/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zhuzi-z/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":6478559908,"node_id":"LA_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABgib6pA","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/labels/RFC","name":"RFC","color":"DE9220","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":9988761457,"node_id":"LA_kwDOI7xefs8AAAACU2BncQ","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/labels/quantization","name":"quantization","color":"62bfab","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":8,"created_at":"2026-10-13T11:25:13Z","updated_at":"2026-10-19T00:47:23Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Motivation.\n\nInference performance depends on the model, target hardware, engine version, parallelism, cache policy, and serving configuration. Exploring this space on GPU clusters is expensive, slow, and difficult to reproduce. Existing CPU-only tests cover isolated components, leaving no low-cost way to reproduce the production engine's end-to-end initialization, scheduling, cache management, and request lifecycle.\n\nThe same limitation also constrains CI coverage. vLLM exposes a large and growing set of serving arguments, but testing their combinations on accelerators is slow and resource-intensive. A CPU execution path that preserves the production control flow would provide the foundation for simulation while also enabling rapid configuration and integration testing.\n\nThis RFC proposes **vLLM Simulator**, with two complementary capabilities:\n\n1. **CPU-based CI with meta tensors.** Build the model and KV cache with meta tensors and run the v1 engine end to end on a CPU host without materializing model-sized storage. This provides the initialization foundation for simulation and lets CI execute the actual model-forward path for structural validation.\n2. **Simulation clock management.** Simulation requests bypass model forward and obtain a hardware-aware step duration from the clock manager. `BLOCKING` waits on wall-clock time for real-time simulation deployment, while `NON_BLOCKING` advances a virtual clock to produce performance metrics without real-time waiting.\n\nThe design follows a clear ownership boundary: reusable CPU-based CI support remains in vLLM, while simulator-specific scheduling, clock management, and workload replay remain external and are integrated through hooks.\n\n### Proposed Change.\n\n#### 1. A reusable CPU-based CI foundation in vLLM\n\nThe CPU-based CI foundation belongs inside vLLM because its meta-tensor execution depends directly on model, operator, and engine shape contracts. Keeping it in-tree allows component owners to update meta support and CI coverage together with production-path changes.\n\nThe proposed `load_format=\"meta\"` path runs the complete eager v1 initialization and request-processing path without real weights or KV storage. The real vLLM configuration, model construction, quantization post-processing, backend selection, KV-cache specification, scheduler, and engine state transitions remain active. Value-dependent outputs such as sampled token IDs are synthetic; numerical and kernel correctness remain the responsibility of hardware tests.\n\nMeta mode skips device-memory profiling and derives its zero-storage KV budget from the model's native KV-cache specifications, sized for the configured sequence and context limits. The standard cache grouping, block calculation, block tables, and scheduler integration therefore remain active without requiring a parallel model-memory estimator.\n\nCPU CI executes the actual model-forward path on meta tensors. Most operators require no meta-specific work: opaque vLLM custom ops use their registered fake implementations, compiled operators whose schemas return nothing or alias an input receive schema-derived meta kernels, and accelerator-only launches are skipped at the launcher when outputs are preallocated. Value-carrying control metadata remains on CPU through request classification, while attention builders project compute-only views to the meta forward device. Explicit handling is limited to independent-output shape rules and host-side logic that reads tensor values, such as weight repacking or metadata construction. Simulation modes bypass model forward at the worker boundary, so they do not intercept or execute lower-level kernels.\n\n#### 2. A non-invasive simulator package\n\nSimulation changes runtime behavior, including request arrival, model-execution timing, and clock progression, so the simulator would be maintained independently under `tools/vllm-simulator`. Import-time hooks around a narrow set of engine, scheduler, executor, and worker seams keep simulation policy outside the vLLM serving path and limit the version-aligned maintenance surface.\n\nThe hooks would be installed before importing vLLM and perform the following functions:\n\n- inject trace or synthetic request arrival times;\n- observe the native scheduler output for each step;\n- convert scheduled requests into predictor inputs such as scheduled-token count and past-KV length;\n- return shape-correct synthetic model outputs; and\n- record per-request and per-iteration metrics.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph META[In-tree CPU-based CI]\n        A[Initialize model with meta tensors]\n        B[Build native KV-cache specifications<br/>from the model configuration]\n        C[Derive the KV budget from per-sequence<br/>requirements and configured limits]\n        D[Allocate native block tables and<br/>the zero-storage meta KV pool]\n        A --> B --> C --> D\n        D --> CI[CPU CI executes the actual<br/>model-forward path on meta]\n        CI --> MK[Registered fakes, schema-derived meta kernels,<br/>metadata projection, and launch-layer skips]\n        MK --> V[Structural validation]\n    end\n\n    subgraph HOOKS[External simulator hook points]\n        H1[EngineCore add-request and step hooks<br/>replay logical request arrivals]\n        H2[Scheduler hooks<br/>keep deferred requests visible]\n        H3[Executor hook<br/>extract the scheduled batch]\n        H4[Worker hook<br/>bypass model forward and return synthetic results]\n        H5[Clock manager<br/>predict and account step latency]\n        H1 --> H2 --> H3 --> H4 --> H5\n    end\n\n    R[Simulation requests] --> H1\n    D --> H2\n    H5 --> O[Native post-step processing and metrics]\n```\n\n#### 3. Simulation clock management\n\nFor every native scheduler step, the external clock manager would build timing input from each request's scheduled-token count and past-KV length. A pluggable hardware-aware predictor would return the step duration, which the clock manager would apply to either the wall clock or the virtual timeline. Time prediction would therefore remain part of clock advancement rather than a separate execution path.\n\nThe initial predictor would use [AIConfigurator](https://github.com/ai-dynamo/aiconfigurator) to map the batch, model configuration, parallel configuration, and target hardware to a per-step duration. Analytical, learned, or replay-based predictors could implement the same clock-facing contract.\n\nPrediction accuracy would be validated against real vLLM deployments using representative prefill, decode, prefix caching, and mixed workloads, with error reported separately for each metric.\n\n#### Execution paths and modes\n\n| Path | Primary purpose | Model and time behavior |\n|---|---|---|\n| CPU-based CI | Structural validation | Execute the actual model-forward path with meta tensors and meta-compatible operators. |\n| `BLOCKING` simulation | Real-time simulation deployment | Bypass model forward and sleep for the predicted step duration. |\n| `NON_BLOCKING` simulation | Main performance-simulation mode | Bypass model forward, replay logical arrivals, and advance a virtual clock. |\n\n#### Application scenarios\n\n- **CPU-based CI:** Rapidly test large combinations of models, serving arguments, and runtime features without consuming accelerator capacity.\n- **Inference-system simulation:** Evaluate performance boundaries and optimization opportunities in a CPU-only environment as inference frameworks and hardware platforms evolve. The simulator can rehearse scheduler, batching, parallelism, and cache-management changes before deployment; predict performance and capacity before new accelerators enter mass production; and analyze KV-cache strategies and ROI across memory and storage tiers.\n\n#### Scope and limits\n\n- Initial scope is the eager v1 engine and text-generation workloads.\n- CPU-based CI validates control flow, tensor shapes, configuration compatibility, and scheduler/cache integration, not numerical correctness or GPU kernel correctness.\n- Parallel configurations (TP, EP, PP, and context parallelism) run as real ranks that form the standard process groups over Gloo. The meta path validates collective control flow but not communication performance; the external simulator accounts for communication cost through its predictor.\n- Prefix caching and CPU-offload control flow are validated without materializing KV payloads or measuring transfer throughput. Network and storage effects are included only when dedicated transfer models are configured.\n- CUDA graph capture, numerical kernel validation, and speculative decoding are outside the initial scope; the speculative acceptance loop depends on token values that meta tensors do not carry.\n- Simulator hooks are version-aligned with vLLM internals; the public serving API remains unchanged when simulation is disabled.\n\n#### Implementation and validation plan\n\nReference implementation:\n\n- CPU-based CI: #61407 \n- Simulator: #61747 \n\n1. Provide meta model loading, device-less platform capabilities, schema-derived operator implementations, forward-device metadata projection, and launch-layer fallbacks required by full eager v1 initialization and generation.\n2. Use the in-tree CPU CI path for meta-based model initialization while keeping scheduler/executor hooks and the clock-facing predictor interface external.\n3. Maintain CPU CI coverage across dense, MoE, hybrid-attention, quantized, TP/EP/PP/context-parallel, prefix caching, and KV-offload configurations through the meta-forward path.\n4. Validate `NON_BLOCKING` predictions against real deployments and publish per-metric error for TTFT, TPOT, throughput, and end-to-end latency.\n\n#### Design questions for maintainers\n\n1. Should full-engine meta execution become a supported CI interface or remain an internal opt-in mode?\n2. Should the fake kernels for independent-output operators be maintained centrally by the CPU CI path or by the owners of the corresponding modules?\n3. Which engine seams are acceptable for an external simulator to hook, and should any become narrow public extension points?\n4. Which additional model and serving-argument combinations should extend the CPU CI matrix?\n5. What accuracy threshold and reference workloads should gate the first performance-simulation release?\n\n\n### Feedback Period.\n\nTwo weeks.\n\n\n### CC List.\n\nModel executor, scheduler, platform, attention, quantization, KV-cache/offload, performance, and CI maintainers.\n\n### Any Other Things.\n\nAI assistance was used to draft this RFC. The human submitter will review and defend every claim and changed line before submission.\n\n### Before submitting a new issue...\n\n- [x] Make sure you already searched for relevant issues, and asked the chatbot living at the bottom right corner of the [documentation page](https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/), which can answer lots of frequently asked questions.","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61406/reactions","total_count":2,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":1,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61406/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61319","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61319/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61319/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61319/events","html_url":"https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61319","id":5823979745,"node_id":"I_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABWyLg4Q","number":61319,"title":"[Bug]:  Greedy (temperature=0) decoding is not reproducible across repeated calls on the same engine for LiquidAI/LFM2-8B-A1B (hybrid mamba)","user":{"login":"jyfct356","id":76062239,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2MDYyMjM5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/76062239?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jyfct356","html_url":"https://github.com/jyfct356","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jyfct356/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jyfct356/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jyfct356/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jyfct356/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jyfct356/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jyfct356/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jyfct356/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jyfct356/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jyfct356/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":5135047172,"node_id":"LA_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABMhKaBA","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/labels/bug","name":"bug","color":"d73a4a","default":true,"description":"Something isn't working","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":9988761457,"node_id":"LA_kwDOI7xefs8AAAACU2BncQ","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/labels/quantization","name":"quantization","color":"62bfab","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":1,"created_at":"2026-10-13T03:27:35Z","updated_at":"2026-10-19T00:43:59Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Your current environment\n\n```\n==============================\n        System Info\n==============================\nOS                           : Ubuntu 22.04.4 LTS (x86_64)\nGCC version                  : (Ubuntu 12.3.0-1ubuntu1~22.04.3) 12.3.0\nClang version                : 17.0.6 (https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project 6009708b4367171ccdbf4b5905cb6a803753fe18)\nCMake version                : version 4.3.4\nLibc version                 : glibc-2.35\n\n=============================