vx f16c rdrand hypervisor lahf_lm abm 3dnowprefetch cpuid_fault ssbd ibrs ibpb stibp ibrs_enhanced tpr_shadow flexpriority ept vpid ept_ad fsgsbase tsc_adjust bmi1 avx2 smep bmi2 erms invpcid avx512f avx512dq rdseed adx smap avx512ifma clflushopt clwb avx512cd sha_ni avx512bw avx512vl xsaveopt xsavec xgetbv1 xsaves user_shstk avx_vnni avx512_bf16 wbnoinvd arat vnmi avx512vbmi umip pku ospke waitpkg avx512_vbmi2 gfni vaes vpclmulqdq avx512_vnni avx512_bitalg avx512_vpopcntdq la57 rdpid bus_lock_detect cldemote movdiri movdir64b fsrm md_clear serialize tsxldtrk ibt amx_bf16 avx512_fp16 amx_tile amx_int8 flush_l1d arch_capabilities\nVirtualization:                          VT-x\nHypervisor vendor:                       KVM\nVirtualization type:                     full\nL1d cache:                               512 KiB (16 instances)\nL1i cache:                               512 KiB (16 instances)\nL2 cache:                                64 MiB (16 instances)\nL3 cache:                                32 MiB (2 instances)\nNUMA node(s):                            2\nNUMA node0 CPU(s):                       0-7\nNUMA node1 CPU(s):                       8-15\nVulnerability Gather data sampling:      Not affected\nVulnerability Ghostwrite:                Not affected\nVulnerability Indirect target selection: Not affected\nVulnerability Itlb multihit:             Not affected\nVulnerability L1tf:                      Not affected\nVulnerability Mds:                       Not affected\nVulnerability Meltdown:                  Not affected\nVulnerability Mmio stale data:           Not affected\nVulnerability Old microcode:             Not affected\nVulnerability Reg file data sampling:    Not affected\nVulnerability Retbleed:                  Not affected\nVulnerability Spec rstack overflow:      Not affected\nVulnerability Spec store bypass:         Mitigation; Speculative Store Bypass disabled via prctl\nVulnerability Spectre v1:                Mitigation; usercopy/swapgs barriers and __user pointer sanitization\nVulnerability Spectre v2:                Mitigation; Enhanced / Automatic IBRS; IBPB conditional; PBRSB-eIBRS SW sequence; BHI BHI_DIS_S\nVulnerability Srbds:                     Not affected\nVulnerability Tsa:                       Not affected\nVulnerability Tsx async abort:           Mitigation; TSX disabled\nVulnerability Vmscape:                   Not affected\n\n==============================\nVersions of relevant libraries\n==============================\n[pip3] flashinfer-python==0.7.0.post1\n[pip3] nccl-extensions==0.1.0\n[pip3] nccl4py==0.6.0\n[pip3] numpy==2.4.6\n[pip3] nvidia-cublas==13.1.1.3\n[pip3] nvidia-cuda-cccl==13.3.4.3.1\n[pip3] nvidia-cuda-crt==13.4.92\n[pip3] nvidia-cuda-cupti==13.0.85\n[pip3] nvidia-cuda-nvcc==13.4.92\n[pip3] nvidia-cuda-nvdisasm==13.4.92\n[pip3] nvidia-cuda-nvrtc==13.0.88\n[pip3] nvidia-cuda-runtime==13.0.96\n[pip3] nvidia-cudnn-cu13==9.20.0.48\n[pip3] nvidia-cudnn-frontend==1.30.0\n[pip3] nvidia-cufft==12.0.0.61\n[pip3] nvidia-cufile==1.15.1.6\n[pip3] nvidia-curand==10.4.0.35\n[pip3] nvidia-cusolver==12.0.4.66\n[pip3] nvidia-cusparse==12.6.3.3\n[pip3] nvidia-cusparselt-cu13==0.8.1\n[pip3] nvidia-cutlass-dsl==4.7.1\n[pip3] nvidia-cutlass-dsl-libs-base==4.7.1\n[pip3] nvidia-cutlass-dsl-libs-core==4.7.1\n[pip3] nvidia-cutlass-dsl-libs-cu12==4.7.1\n[pip3] nvidia-cutlass-dsl-libs-cu13==4.7.1\n[pip3] nvidia-ml-py==13.615.71\n[pip3] nvidia-nccl-cu13==2.29.7\n[pip3] nvidia-nvjitlink==13.4.92\n[pip3] nvidia-nvshmem-cu13==3.4.5\n[pip3] nvidia-nvtx==13.0.85\n[pip3] nvidia-nvvm==13.4.92\n[pip3] pyzmq==27.2.0\n[pip3] tokenspeed-triton==3.8.10.post20260920\n[pip3] torch==2.13.0\n[pip3] torch-c-dlpack-ext==0.1.5\n[pip3] torchaudio==2.11.0\n[pip3] torchcodec==0.17.0\n[pip3] torchvision==0.28.0\n[pip3] transformers==5.17.0\n[pip3] triton==3.7.1\n[conda] No relevant packages\n\n==============================\n         vLLM Info\n==============================\nROCM Version                 : Could not collect\nvLLM Version                 : 0.31.0\nvLLM Build Flags:\n  CUDA Archs: Not Set; ROCm: Disabled; XPU: Disabled\nGPU Topology:\n  \tGPU0\tGPU1\tGPU2\tGPU3\tCPU Affinity\tNUMA Affinity\tGPU NUMA ID\nGPU0\t X \tNV6\tNV6\tNV6\t0-15\t0-1\t\tN/A\nGPU1\tNV6\t X \tNV6\tNV6\t0-15\t0-1\t\tN/A\nGPU2\tNV6\tNV6\t X \tNV6\t0-15\t0-1\t\tN/A\nGPU3\tNV6\tNV6\tNV6\t X \t0-15\t0-1\t\tN/A\n\nLegend:\n\n  X    = Self\n  SYS  = Connection traversing PCIe as well as the SMP interconnect between NUMA nodes (e.g., QPI/UPI)\n  NODE = Connection traversing PCIe as well as the interconnect between PCIe Host Bridges within a NUMA node\n  PHB  = Connection traversing PCIe as well as a PCIe Host Bridge (typically the CPU)\n  PXB  = Connection traversing multiple PCIe bridges (without traversing the PCIe Host Bridge)\n  PIX  = Connection traversing at most a single PCIe bridge\n  NV#  = Connection traversing a bonded set of # NVLinks\n\n==============================\n     Environment Variables\n==============================\nPYTORCH_NVML_BASED_CUDA_CHECK=1\nTORCHINDUCTOR_COMPILE_THREADS=1\nTORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR=/tmp/torchinductor_oscar\n\n```\n\n</details>\n\n- vLLM: 0.30.0 (`vllm/vllm-openai:latest`, also tested on nightly: <yes/no, result>)\n- speculators: 0.9.0a67 (drafter created with `speculators train --speculator-type dflash`)\n- GPU: NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3\n\n### 🐛 Describe the bug\n\nServing `openai/gpt-oss-20b` with a DFlash drafter crashes during engine warmup with an illegal memory access inside the FlashAttention 3 forward kernel. The crash happens with both an untrained drafter (from `speculators train --dry-run`) and a trained one, so it isn't caused by the weights.\n\n**Command**\n\nRun in the `vllm/vllm-openai:latest` Docker image (vLLM 0.30.0) on a single GPU, with `CUDA_LAUNCH_BLOCKING=1` set to get an accurate error location:\n\n```bash\nCUDA_LAUNCH_BLOCKING=1 vllm serve openai/gpt-oss-20b \\\n  --served-model-name openai/gpt-oss-20b \\\n  --enforce-eager \\\n  --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80 \\\n  --max-model-len 8192 \\\n  --max-num-seqs 16 \\\n  --max-num-batched-tokens 8192 \\\n  --speculative-config '{\"method\": \"dflash\", \"model\": \"/path/to/dflash-drafter\", \"num_speculative_tokens\": 15}'\n```\n\nThe drafter was created with `speculators train --speculator-type dflash --verifier-name-or-path openai/gpt-oss-20b --dry-run` (speculators 0.9.0a67), then rebuilt with intermediate_size changed to 8192, so its weights are untrained. Its `config.json` is attached below. A trained drafter crashes the same way, so the weights don't matter.\n\n**Error** (with `CUDA_LAUNCH_BLOCKING=1` and `--enforce-eager`)\n\n```\nCUDA error (/workspace/.deps/vllm-flash-attn-src/hopper/flash_fwd_launch_template.h:171): an illegal memory access was encountered\n```\n\nWithout `CUDA_LAUNCH_BLOCKING=1` (and with CUDA graphs enabled), the error is reported asynchronously during `compile_or_warm_up_model` as a device-side assert, pointing at `self.draft_tokens[req_idx].zero_()` in the DFlash proposer. That location is probably not where the failure actually happens.\n\n**Drafter config** (DFlash, built against gpt-oss-20b)\n\n- 5 layers, `block_size` 16, `hidden_size` 2880, `head_dim` 64, 64 query heads, 8 KV heads, `intermediate_size` 8192\n- Full vocabulary (`draft_vocab_size` 201088, no d2t/t2d), `mask_token_id` 200019\n- `aux_hidden_state_layer_ids` [2, 12, 21], plus the last layer for targets\n- Default drafter attention layout: sliding window 2048, with layer 2 set to full attention\n\n<details><summary>Drafter config.json</summary>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"architectures\": [\n    \"DFlashDraftModel\"\n  ],\n  \"auto_map\": {\n    \"\": \"config.DFlashSpeculatorConfig\"\n  },\n  \"aux_hidden_state_layer_ids\": [\n    2,\n    12,\n    21\n  ],\n  \"block_size\": 16,\n  \"draft_vocab_size\": 201088,\n  \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n  \"mask_token_id\": 200019,\n  \"sample_from_anchor\": false,\n  \"sliding_window_non_causal\": false,\n  \"speculators_config\": {\n    \"algorithm\": \"dflash\",\n    \"default_proposal_method\": \"greedy\",\n    \"proposal_methods\": [\n      {\n        \"accept_tolerance\": 0.0,\n        \"proposal_type\": \"greedy\",\n        \"speculative_tokens\": 15,\n        \"verifier_accept_k\": 1\n      }\n    ],\n    \"verifier\": {\n      \"architectures\": [\n        \"GptOssForCausalLM\"\n      ],\n      \"name_or_path\": \"openai/gpt-oss-20b\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"speculators_model_type\": \"dflash\",\n  \"speculators_version\": \"0.9.0a67\",\n  \"target_hidden_size\": null,\n  \"tie_word_embeddings\": false,\n  \"transformer_layer_config\": {\n    \"attention_bias\": false,\n    \"attention_dropout\": 0.0,\n    \"bos_token_id\": null,\n    \"eos_token_id\": null,\n    \"head_dim\": 64,\n    \"hidden_act\": \"silu\",\n    \"hidden_size\": 2880,\n    \"initializer_range\": 0.02,\n    \"intermediate_size\": 8192,\n    \"layer_types\": [\n      \"sliding_attention\",\n      \"sliding_attention\",\n      \"full_attention\",\n      \"sliding_attention\",\n      \"sliding_attention\"\n    ],\n    \"max_position_embeddings\": 131072,\n    \"max_window_layers\": 28,\n    \"model_type\": \"qwen3\",\n    \"num_attention_heads\": 64,\n    \"num_hidden_layers\": 5,\n    \"num_key_value_heads\": 8,\n    \"pad_token_id\": null,\n    \"rms_norm_eps\": 0.00001,\n    \"rope_parameters\": {\n      \"beta_fast\": 32.0,\n      \"beta_slow\": 1.0,\n      \"factor\": 32.0,\n      \"original_max_position_embeddings\": 4096,\n      \"rope_theta\": 150000,\n      \"rope_type\": \"yarn\",\n      \"truncate\": false\n    },\n    \"sliding_window\": 2048,\n    \"tie_word_embeddings\": false,\n    \"use_cache\": true,\n    \"use_sliding_window\": true,\n    \"vocab_size\": 201088\n  },\n  \"transformers_version\": \"5.16.1\"\n}\n\n```\n</details>\n\n### What we tried\n\n| Configuration | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `method: ngram`, `num_speculative_tokens: 15` (same target, same flags) | ✅ Works. Target verification of 16-token blocks on FA3 is fine. |\n| DFlash drafter with **all layers full attention** (no sliding window) | ❌ Same FA3 illegal memory access |\n| `--disable-hybrid-kv-cache-manager` | Not applicable. Fails with `All attention layers in the same KV cache group must have the same window size` because gpt-oss itself mixes 128-token sliding-window and full attention. |\n| `--speculative-config.attention_backend FLEX_ATTENTION` + `--enforce-eager` | ✅ Works. Outputs match the non-speculative server at temperature 0 (apart from rare late divergence), and a drafter trained with speculators gets non-zero acceptance. |\n\nThe crash therefore seems specific to the DFlash drafter's attention running on FA3 when the target is gpt-oss. Our guess, not verified, is that it comes from the drafter's non-causal attention interacting with gpt-oss's hybrid KV-cache layout (sliding-window plus full-attention groups, attention sinks) when the attention metadata is built.\n\n### Before submitting a new issue...\n\n- [x] Make sure you already searched for relevant issues, and asked the chatbot living at the bottom right corner of the [documentation page](https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/), which can answer lots of frequently asked questions.","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61330/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61330/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62355","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62355/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62355/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62355/events","html_url":"https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62355","id":5914929080,"node_id":"I_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABYI6nuA","number":62355,"title":"[Performance]: Whisper log-mel runs on the renderer thread instead of the GPU worker","user":{"login":"Aravind-11","id":42345018,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQyMzQ1MDE4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/42345018?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11","html_url":"https://github.com/Aravind-11","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-10-18T22:41:48Z","updated_at":"2026-10-18T23:31:34Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Proposal to improve performance\n\nWhisper publishes `input_features` (`whisper.py`, about line 780). `embed_multimodal` forwards them into `get_encoder_outputs` (about lines 1003–1007). The renderer runs that processor on `_mm_executor` with `max_workers=1` (`renderers/base.py`, about lines 148 and 937).\n\nRead at `9c778e9ff8d0c78a241d7f530c441c2dc1d67533`. Still present on main `e0adacc9a67a5f2b2d8ba508f7f522bc1ff83c6d` (2026-10-18).\n\n`WhisperFeatureExtractor` builds log-mel with `_torch_extract_fbank_features` at `device=\"cpu\"`, inside the renderer's single multimodal thread, under `set_default_torch_num_threads()` (`OMP_NUM_THREADS`, or else 1). `_postprocess_output` then casts that float tensor to the model dtype. The worker receives log-mel and starts at `conv1`.\n\nThe log-mel already matches the extractor to about `1e-5`. This moves that same STFT onto the GPU worker. Whisper only.\n\nThe STT pool (`VLLM_MAX_AUDIO_PREPROCESS_WORKERS`) only decodes and chunks. Its default is `min(cpu_count, 2)`, and the env var can already raise it. That pool leaves the STFT where it is. Voxtral already runs its own STFT on the worker and stays unchanged.\n\n**Proposed change.** Same commit: ship float32 PCM, salt the processor-cache key, and run the extractor formula on the worker. API servers and Whisper workers roll onto that commit together.\n\n1. `WhisperMultiModalProcessor` stops calling the Hugging Face extractor. The resampled mono waveform becomes passthrough `audio_pcm`: CPU float32, `batched(\"audio\")`, `keep_on_cpu` left false. The processor stops publishing `input_features`.\n2. `audio_pcm` stays out of the `BatchFeature` that `call_hf_processor` post-processes. `_postprocess_output` runs only there (`context.py` 437 and 447). `_finalize_hf_mm_data` merges passthrough afterward (`processor.py` 1312) and leaves the PCM out of that cast. A later edit that routes the field through `_postprocess_output` exempts it in that same commit.\n3. Prompt length stays `WhisperProcessingInfo.get_num_audio_tokens()` == `max_source_positions`. `get_num_audio_tokens(duration)` stays the streaming usage counter only. It is not the placeholder length.\n4. Salt `audio_ipc_schema=\"whisper_pcm_f32_v1\"` into Whisper hash factors only, on a copy the extractor does not see. With no UUID, the hash is model id + resampled PCM + kwargs, including that key. With a UUID, `get_mm_hashes` hashes the UUID string plus those factors, not the PCM bytes (`inputs.py` 91–94). The key makes `has_hash_factors` true, so a bare UUID misses an unsalted entry.\n5. The rollout that ships the commit clears unsalted entries: `clear_mm_cache`, `_mm_processor_only_cache`, worker `encoder_cache`, and EC `cross_encoder_cache` embeddings stored without that key.\n6. `WhisperForConditionalGeneration.embed_multimodal` runs the formula below in float32 on `conv1.weight.device`, then casts to `conv1.weight.dtype`, then calls `get_encoder_outputs`. `WhisperEncoder.forward` stays `gelu(conv1)` then `gelu(conv2)`. A batch that still has log-mel `input_features` and no `audio_pcm` raises.\n\nThe STFT lives in `embed_multimodal`, not in `WhisperEncoder.forward`. `UltravoxWhisperEncoder.forward` replaces that method, skips `super()`, and still expects mel.\n\nPer clip, constants from the loaded extractor:\n\n1. Pad with `padding_value` or truncate to `n_samples = chunk_length * sampling_rate`. `do_normalize` stays off.\n2. Hann window of length `n_fft` on the conv device.\n3. Dither (`std = dither`) only when `extractor.dither != 0`.\n4. `torch.stft(waveform, n_fft, hop_length, window=window, return_complex=True)`. The call keeps the extractor defaults for `center`, `pad_mode`, `win_length`, `normalized`, and `onesided`. `center=True` stays.\n5. `magnitudes = (stft[..., :-1].abs() ** 2).contiguous()`. This is transformers 5.16.1 and 5.19.0. `.contiguous()` leaves the values unchanged.\n6. Slaney mel filters, `1 + n_fft // 2` bins, 0–8000 Hz. `mel_spec = filters.T @ magnitudes`.\n7. `clamp(min=1e-10).log10()`, then per-clip max−8 (max over time, then over bins, per row). Each clip has its own max.\n8. `(log_spec + 4) / 4`, then cast to `conv1.weight.dtype`.\n\nMel time is `n_samples // hop_length` (stock 3000), so conv2 yields `max_source_positions` (stock 1500). Compare GPU STFT to CPU `_torch_extract_fbank_features` at about `1e-5`. GPU `torch.stft` matches within that tolerance and is not bit-identical. `torch.stft` stays eager, outside any encoder CUDA graph.\n\n`return_attention_mask` hop-strides only `attention_mask`. It leaves `input_features` uncut. Whisper never reads that mask. Stock 480000 is divisible by hop 160, so the extra mask drop is unused.\n\nOut of scope for this change:\n\n- Raising `VLLM_MAX_AUDIO_PREPROCESS_WORKERS`, `renderer_num_workers`, `_mm_executor`, or OMP threads. `set_torch_threads_for_runtime` wraps other calls. It returns without changing the count when `OMP_NUM_THREADS` was set outside vLLM.\n- `mm_processor_kwargs[\"device\"]=\"cuda\"`. An accelerator processor is rejected unless the instance is encode-only, and it would still run STFT in the API process.\n- Qwen2-Audio, Qwen3-ASR, Ultravox, GLM-ASR, and Moss. They share the placement and have different prompt math. Moss per-clip tokens come from `audio_data_seqlens` (`mel.shape[-1]` and `conv3_downsample_len`). `get_mm_max_tokens_per_item` is the 30-second hop ceiling, not the clip's mel length.\n- Voxtral. Its STFT is already on the worker.\n\n### Report of performance regression\n\nNone. Outputs stay within about `1e-5` of the CPU extractor. The move is off the renderer's single multimodal thread and onto the GPU worker.\n\n### Misc discussion on performance\n\nThe worker already owns the encoder. The STFT is the piece still stuck on `_mm_executor` (`max_workers=1`). Shipping PCM instead of log-mel is what makes that move possible without changing prompt length.\n\n### Your current environment (if you think it is necessary)\n\nSource read on the commits above, against transformers 5.16.1 and 5.19.0. No full `collect_env.py` run. The `1e-5` figure is the existing extractor agreement, not a new GPU measurement.\n\n### Checks\n\n- Short, exact-`n_samples`, and over-long clips match CPU `_torch_extract_fbank_features` at about `1e-5`. Shared noise when dither is nonzero. Two clips in one batch each match their own max.\n- `WhisperMultiModalProcessor` leaves `WhisperFeatureExtractor.__call__` unused. `audio_pcm` stays float32 through the passthrough merge.\n- The schema key changes the no-UUID hash. A UUID hash is the UUID plus the factors, and it misses a bare-UUID entry. Replaying an unsalted log-mel entry leaves `audio_pcm` empty.\n- Log-mel `input_features` with no `audio_pcm` raises in `embed_multimodal`.\n- Ultravox still gelu-convs mel and still raises `Make sure to pad the input mel`.\n- A 1 s clip and a 30 s clip both produce an encoder prompt of length `max_source_positions`.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62355/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62355/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62347","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62347/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62347/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62347/events","html_url":"https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62347","id":5914706920,"node_id":"I_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABYItD6A","number":62347,"title":"[Bug]: Qwen3-ASR realtime flush emits the tail as a fresh ChatML prompt with no left audio","user":{"login":"Aravind-11","id":42345018,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQyMzQ1MDE4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/42345018?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11","html_url":"https://github.com/Aravind-11","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Aravind-11/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-10-18T22:12:55Z","updated_at":"2026-10-18T23:31:27Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Your current environment\n\nRead in `vllm/model_executor/models/qwen3_asr_realtime.py` at `9c778e9ff8d0c78a241d7f530c441c2dc1d67533`. Still present on main `e0adacc9a67a5f2b2d8ba508f7f522bc1ff83c6d` (2026-10-18).\n\nNo `collect_env.py` output. The failure is in how the flush prompt is built, before any GPU forward.\n\n### 🐛 Describe the bug\n\n`read_audio` emits a fixed 5.0 s segment and leaves the remainder in the buffer. `flush` copies only that remainder. `buffer_realtime_audio` then yields it as a new ChatML `TokensPrompt` whose `multi_modal_data[\"audio\"]` is that short slice alone.\n\nThe tower treats a zero remainder as a full chunk:\n\n```python\nchunk_lengths[tail_chunk_index] = feature_lens % (self.n_window * 2)\nchunk_lengths[chunk_lengths == 0] = self.n_window * 2\n```\n\nThe flush therefore starts a new window on the tail and omits the left context that chunk already covered.\n\nSeparate from this bug: text/KV carry (#56218, #51374), the language-header leak (#55786), session `prompt`/`language` (#61026), and KV reuse (#51037).\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe 5.0 s gate stays as it is. Each `read_audio` segment stays exactly `segment_size` samples. After a segment is returned, the buffer remembers only that segment's last tower chunk and prepends it once on the next non-empty flush.\n\n```text\nchunk_frames = n_window * 2          # loaded audio_config.n_window\nrem_frames   = feature_lens % chunk_frames\nif rem_frames == 0:\n    rem_frames = chunk_frames\ncarry_samples = rem_frames * hop_length\n```\n\n`hop_length` and `feature_lens` come from the loaded feature extractor, on that hop grid. The carry is the trailing `carry_samples` of the segment just yielded.\n\nA 5.0 s segment is 80000 samples. Hop 160 makes 500 mel frames.\n\n- Checkpoint `n_window=50` (`chunk_frames=100`): `500 % 100 == 0`, so the last chunk is 100 frames. Carry = 16000 samples.\n- In-tree default `n_window=100` (`chunk_frames=200`): `500 % 200 == 100`. Carry is also 16000 samples. The last chunk starts at frame 400. A fixed `2 * n_window * hop` prefix is 32000 samples and starts at frame 300, inside the finished chunk.\n\nRules that follow from the tower:\n\n- A stream that never filled one segment has an empty carry. `flush` returns the tail unchanged.\n- `flush` of an empty buffer stays `None`. A held carry is not a prompt by itself.\n- A non-empty `flush` returns `concat(carry, tail)` once, then clears the carry. A second `flush` is `None`.\n- Later `read_audio` segments stay exactly `segment_size`. The client already appends deltas, so the carry belongs on the flush only.\n- The carry width is `rem_frames * hop_length` from the loaded `n_window`. `n_window_infer` is a different field.\n- The ChatML `prompt_token_ids` stay the same template. The extra audio is only in `multi_modal_data[\"audio\"]`.\n\nThe token delta is whatever `_get_feat_extract_output_lengths` returns for the flush mel length minus the tail-only mel length. `+13` happens only when those two mel lengths differ by 100. Mel length is the extractor's attention-mask sum, not `len(samples) / hop`.\n\n- `Qwen3ASRFeatureExtractor` (`min_length=8000`, pad counted in the mask): 1600 samples can be 50 frames / 7 tokens, and 17600 samples can be 110 frames / 15 tokens. Delta `+8`.\n- `WhisperFeatureExtractor` (what Qwen/Qwen3-ASR ships; no `min_length`): `+13` only after the two measured mask sums differ by 100.\n\nOfficial `finish_streaming_transcribe` re-decodes the whole waveform. This change leaves that path alone. Conv outputs at the junction will differ from that re-decode. There is no text-prefix rollback.\n\nOut of scope: the 5.0 s gate, reading `input_stream`, and echo cancellation.\n\n### Checks\n\nBuffer only, no weights. `sampling_rate=16000`, hop 160.\n\n- Under 80000 samples: `read_audio` is `None`. `flush` equals those samples.\n- Exactly 80000: one `read_audio` of 80000. `flush` is `None` even though a carry was saved.\n- 80000 + 1600, both `n_window=50` and `n_window=100`: `read_audio` is the first 80000 unchanged. `flush` has length 17600 and starts with `segment[-16000:]`. A 32000-sample prefix fails this check.\n- Two full segments plus a tail: the carry is the end of the second segment only.\n- A second `flush` after a non-empty flush is `None`.\n- Token check uses the real extractor. `Qwen3ASRFeatureExtractor`: 1600 vs 17600 can be 7 and 15 tokens (`+8`). `WhisperFeatureExtractor`: `+13` only when the measured mel lengths differ by 100.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62347/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62347/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61024","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61024/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61024/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61024/events","html_url":"https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61024","id":5794494700,"node_id":"I_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABWWD47A","number":61024,"title":"[Roadmap] vLLM Roadmap Q4 2026","user":{"login":"simon-mo","id":21118851,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIxMTE4ODUx","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/21118851?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/simon-mo","html_url":"https://github.com/simon-mo","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/simon-mo/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/simon-mo/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/simon-mo/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/simon-mo/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/simon-mo/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/simon-mo/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/simon-mo/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/simon-mo/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/simon-mo/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":6114440385,"node_id":"LA_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABbHL0wQ","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/labels/rocm","name":"rocm","color":"B60205","default":false,"description":"Related to AMD ROCm","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":9988761457,"node_id":"LA_kwDOI7xefs8AAAACU2BncQ","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/labels/quantization","name":"quantization","color":"62bfab","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":11639749480,"node_id":"LA_kwDOI7xefs8AAAACtch7aA","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/labels/kimi","name":"kimi","color":"78d0bd","default":false,"description":null,"archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":11639749575,"node_id":"LA_kwDOI7xefs8AAAACtch7xw","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/labels/k3","name":"k3","color":"e429eb","default":false,"description":null,"archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":5,"created_at":"2026-10-10T19:21:47Z","updated_at":"2026-10-18T20:39:46Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"COLLABORATOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"In [#32455](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/32455), we broke down vLLM's goals into various special interest groups (SIGs). Building on the [Q3 roadmap](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/48168), please find below each SIG's Q4 roadmap. You can find regular meetings of these SIGs on the public vLLM calendar.\n\n## Key takeaway\n\n* **Agentic Workload:** Q3 established Model Runner V2 as the default and brought production-ready Rust frontend and distributed KV offloading support. Q4 focuses on making optimized agentic serving easier to deploy: better day-0 performance, tuned production recipes, adaptive speculative decoding, richer agent hints, and fault tolerance across hardware.\n* **Ecosystem:** We are expanding low-precision serving and checkpoint conversion across NVIDIA and AMD, strengthening speculative decoding and RL integration, and advancing Agentic API and vLLM-Omni. Frontier models and new hardware need a clear path from initial support to validated, optimized production configurations.\n* **OSS Maintenance:** We are simplifying engine and model interfaces, completing quantization dispatch refactors, and improving CI coverage, time to signal, security, and performance evaluation. Agent-assisted review and better test-quality checks should help the community maintain a growing model, feature, and hardware matrix.\n\n### SIG Core ([Meeting Notes](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1x36yhn655LiuV6bPK2C1M-6jfMzgXDLQf2PIngJndgc/edit?tab=t.0))\n\n- [ ] Fully remove Model Runner V1 and close the remaining [Model Runner V2 gaps](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/47172).\n- [ ] Implement [extensible KV cache support](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/56492), pluggable `KVCacheConfigBuilder` implementations for models, and better cache-grouping heuristics for sliding-window attention speculators.\n- [ ] Refactor CUDA graphs: separate prefill/decode graph sizes; clarify capture, dispatch, and padding semantics, including adaptive verification and sequence parallelism; and simplify encoder and DeepSeek V4.1 prefill-skipping graphs. Validate prefill graph sizing against padding overhead and performance regressions.\n- [ ] Simplify and harden the engine's model loop, data-parallel flow, and scheduler, and improve streaming-input reliability.\n- [ ] Reduce coupling between model implementations and the engine through a clearer model interface.\n- [ ] Advance the Rust frontend toward default use, including resolving Responses API readiness and parity requirements.\n- [ ] Add native per-step observability through [forward-pass metrics](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/52061), in collaboration with Dynamo.\n\n* Aspirational work:\n  - Simplify KV connector interfaces through standardized KV layouts, including exploring parallel interfaces.\n  - Revisit automatic prefix caching and investigate lookahead EAGLE block caching as a replacement for block dropping.\n  - Explore a more comprehensive scheduler redesign beyond the immediate cleanup.\n\n### SIG Large Scale Serving ([Meeting Notes](https://docs.google.com/document/d/15A9zid75c41pcn8hzIKXOW2R6VFgeUfGuIMq-ebgEEI/edit?tab=t.0#heading=h.prqroxfe0fr1))\n\n- [ ] Extend agent hints across routers and KV connectors with Dynamo and llm-d: multi-tenancy, cache-tier TTLs, filesystem eviction, and request-level control of prefetching, eviction, and selective offloading.\n- [ ] Publish production recipes with clear explanations of deployment and performance tradeoffs, including disaggregated prefill/decode and KV offloading.\n- [ ] Harden numerical fault handling, including NaNs, and expert-parallel recovery through restart and scale-down; integrate recovery with llm-d and Dynamo.\n- [ ] Achieve state-of-the-art agentic workload performance for priority models across AMD and new hardware, including Rubin and MI455X.\n- [ ] Integrate native [forward-pass metrics](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/52061) into the serving observability work with Dynamo.\n\n* Proposed work: define the scope and milestones for AFD.\n\n### SIG Model Performance ([Meeting Notes](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1A1TOuCTPQ_72npOSzhWKfYOmDIF7OoagLGaL8gHe1TQ/edit?tab=t.0#heading=h.wmm9txlfqo9w))\n\n- [ ] Improve day-0 performance and the workflow from initial model support to optimized agentic serving configurations.\n- [ ] Continue sprints for frontier models, including DeepSeek V4.1, GLM 5.3, and Qwen3.8, with continuous model and feature tracking.\n- [ ] Optimize Vera Rubin performance and publish reproducible serving recipes.\n- [ ] Expand FP4 KV cache support to more models and add disk-backed KV offloading.\n- [ ] Continue the [performance-evaluation dashboard](https://vllm-ci-dashboard.vercel.app/perf) and broaden regression tracking with SIG CI.\n\n* Proposals to evaluate: bring `vllm-recipes` into the main repository, restructure model-specific tests, and clarify the scope and feasibility of deprecating vLLM IR and custom Torch compile passes.\n\n### SIG Spec Decode ([Meeting Notes](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1DlgW4dWZyGup_BOZm24Bo50AFIiQgPXfSLqMGDLt6Ic/edit?tab=t.0#heading=h.1efi34fxtsr2))\n\n- [ ] Improve HMA and heterogeneous attention support between drafter and verifier. Make sliding-window drafters compose efficiently with different target attention types and reduce KV cache padding and memory overhead.\n- [ ] Polish adaptive verification and extend support to Mamba2, GDN, and QSA. Research oracle verification to quantify remaining performance opportunities and guide optimization.\n- [ ] Migrate speculative model code to Flat Model, then optimize fusion and kernels for low-latency inference, including rejection sampling.\n- [ ] Enable block rejection sampling and probabilistic verification by default.\n- [ ] Finalize `open-spec-config` configuration details for popular draft algorithms and implement draft-model loading.\n- [ ] Improve configuration ergonomics and robustness with tool calling, reasoning, and structured outputs. Enabling speculative decoding itself by default remains an aspiration.\n\n### SIG CI ([Meeting Notes](https://docs.google.com/document/u/2/d/18VJOuEyAZmRVILWpOtYx5jC4iFZLwjHVw5TAdfJnjXQ/edit?tab=t.0))\n\n- [ ] Collect test-coverage data and maintain a feature-combination matrix, including prefill/decode disaggregation.\n- [ ] Bring time to signal toward 30 minutes through automatic test selection, pytest-level granularity, automatic sharding, and default job timeouts.\n- [ ] Migrate remaining four-L4 jobs to H200 MIG, reduce Docker build/pull and environment-setup overhead, and retire obsolete jobs for old models.\n- [ ] Improve infrastructure-failure detection, quarantine, and retries, and carry forward flaky-test management.\n- [ ] Perform offensive security testing of vLLM and make CI safe for projects throughout the vLLM ecosystem.\n- [ ] Expand performance and accuracy evaluation to disaggregated serving and TPU, including InferenceX-style benchmark sweeps and throughput/latency Pareto curves.\n- [ ] Develop agent-assisted reviewers and CI shepherds, with checks for unit-test quality.\n- [ ] Clarify and document CI trigger paths and coverage for RDNA, XPU, and other accelerators, including projects maintained outside the main repository, to reduce cross-backend breakage.\n\n### SIG Quantization\n\n- [ ] **LUT-B (W3A8) on Rubin:** finalize the NVIDIA format specification, including tile sizes, codebooks, and scales ([Model Optimizer #2204](https://github.com/NVIDIA/Model-Optimizer/issues/2204), [compressed-tensors #801](https://github.com/vllm-project/compressed-tensors/pull/801)); provide an [LLM Compressor calibration recipe](https://github.com/vllm-project/llm-compressor/pull/3018), validated checkpoints, and optimized dense/MoE kernels with MXFP8 activations.\n- [ ] **Production FP4 and lower-bit KV caches:** publish accuracy evaluations and recommended configurations for priority models. Cover long-context interactive serving and high-concurrency data generation, and compose with hybrid attention, speculative drafters, NIXL disaggregation, and tiered offloading.\n  - Expand NVFP4 coverage across FlashMLA/DSA sparse attention, FA4, Rubin kernels, and [SM8x–SM12x enablement](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/46963).\n  - Advance ROCm low-bit KV work, including [UltraQuant](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/57057) and the [Octave KV proposal](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/59773), with platform-specific validation rather than assuming one format fits every backend.\n- [ ] **Checkpoint requantization:** complete the [online quantization from arbitrary precision roadmap](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/59062) through a common online quantization API, including [requantization](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/55684), [activation QuantKey overrides](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/51808), [shared experts](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/55686), and [MLA `kv_b_proj`](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/55741). Initial conversions include MXFP8 to FP8 per-channel/block on Hopper and MI300, and FP8/NVFP4 to MXFP4 across vendors.\n- [ ] **Unified dispatch and inspectability:** finish QuantKey/QuantConfig adoption in [Quark](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/56099), [ModelOpt MoE](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/55339), and compressed-tensors. Declare weight and activation QuantKeys across methods, complete the [MoE oracle and linear-kernel migration](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/54959), extend QuantKey into `FusedMoEQuantDesc`, and log selected kernels/backends by layer type.\n- [ ] **Kernel consolidation:** make [Humming the default over Marlin on Hopper](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/56997), then extend to SM10x/SM12x. Consolidate autotuning configurations and benchmark overlapping CuTe-DSL, b12x, FlashInfer TRTLLM, Marlin, Qutlass, and Humming paths to choose per-architecture defaults and retire superseded paths.\n- [ ] **Compression ecosystem:** deliver day-0 NVFP4/MXFP4 checkpoints for priority models, advance [MoNe](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/50415) and [REAP expert pruning](https://github.com/vllm-project/llm-compressor/pull/2906), and make `convert_checkpoint` the general format-conversion entry point.\n- [ ] **Quality validation:** add quantized-model accuracy CI, tensor-parallel parity checks for online quantization, and quantized weight-reload correctness checks with the RL ecosystem.\n\n* Research directions: LUT-B attention support, FP4 with rotations through QuTLASS, HIGGS, and QAD. Continue evaluating [legacy format deprecation](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/30136).\n\n### SIG AMD\n\n- [ ] Close frontier-model prefill, decode, speculative decoding, and long-context performance gaps on ROCm, prioritizing DeepSeek V4/V4.1, Kimi K3, GLM 5.x, and Qwen3.x.\n- [ ] Productionize context parallelism for sparse, MLA, and hybrid-attention models, and harden prefill/decode disaggregation and KV transfer.\n- [ ] Improve multi-tier KV offloading across GPU, host memory, SSD, and distributed storage, including Mooncake, MoRI/UMBP, and NIXL integrations.\n- [ ] Expand AITER kernels and fused FP8/MXFP8/FP4/MXFP4 execution paths, with automatic backend selection and fewer AMD-specific configuration requirements.\n- [ ] Strengthen AMD/Quark day-0 support: FP4 and FP8-PTPC for priority CDNA models, plus INT4 and other low-bit formats for priority RDNA models. Coordinate online requantization and flexible activation QuantKey overrides with SIG Quantization.\n- [ ] Advance UltraQuant and MXFP4 KV-cache/attention support on ROCm, and support MTP, EAGLE, and DFlash for priority models, including quantized draft heads.\n- [ ] Maintain MI355X as the primary performance platform while closing MI300X/MI325X gaps, expanding RDNA support where applicable, and improving AMD CI parity, performance regression coverage, and ROCm release readiness. Document the RDNA validation and CI trigger path with SIG CI.\n\n### SIG Agentic API\n\n- [ ] Deliver [multi-agent APIs](https://github.com/vllm-project/agentic-api/issues/298).\n- [ ] Add a [code interpreter tool](https://github.com/vllm-project/agentic-api/issues/308).\n- [ ] Use the [shared Rust frontend library](https://github.com/vllm-project/agentic-api/issues/359) for direct vLLM engine inference.\n- [ ] Add a [file search tool](https://github.com/vllm-project/agentic-api/pull/278).\n\n### vLLM-Omni\n\n- [ ] Continue optimizing frontier diffusion models, including MiniMax H3.\n- [ ] Integrate with RL frameworks, including verl with ve-omni as the training framework.\n- [ ] Expand acceleration backends and framework integrations, including FastVideo.\n- [ ] Complete the [full-duplex redesign](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm-omni/issues/7181), carrying forward Q3 prototyping work.\n\n### RL Ecosystem\n\n- [ ] Execute the [Q4 vLLM × RL roadmap](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/58990): weight updates, training–inference consistency, and feature enablement such as LoRA for RL.\n- [ ] Improve day-0 RL support for new models.\n- [ ] Refactor weight updates and advance the auxiliary connector design to address correctness at the architectural level, including quantized weight reloads with SIG Quantization.\n\n------\n\nPlease leave feedback and comments, and work directly with the relevant SIG areas for deeper collaboration.","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61024/reactions","total_count":10,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":10,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61024/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62130","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62130/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62130/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62130/events","html_url":"https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62130","id":5894601292,"node_id":"I_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABX1h6TA","number":62130,"title":"[Spec Decode] Compose grammar-constrained sampling with EAGLE speculative decoding","user":{"login":"YuvrajSinghBhadoria2","id":245782664,"node_id":"U_kgDODqZYiA","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/245782664?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/YuvrajSinghBhadoria2","html_url":"https://github.com/YuvrajSinghBhadoria2","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YuvrajSinghBhadoria2/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YuvrajSinghBhadoria2/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YuvrajSinghBhadoria2/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YuvrajSinghBhadoria2/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YuvrajSinghBhadoria2/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YuvrajSinghBhadoria2/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YuvrajSinghBhadoria2/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YuvrajSinghBhadoria2/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YuvrajSinghBhadoria2/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":6467979044,"node_id":"LA_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABgYWHJA","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/labels/structured-output","name":"structured-output","color":"4cb782","default":false,"description":null,"archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":6915642480,"node_id":"LA_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABnDRUcA","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/labels/speculative-decoding","name":"speculative-decoding","color":"6A493C","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":2,"created_at":"2026-10-17T03:57:24Z","updated_at":"2026-10-18T17:21:12Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"## Problem\n\nSpeculative decoding and grammar-constrained sampling (`structured_outputs`,\nguided decoding) do not compose in vLLM today. Under structured outputs the\ntarget verifier only accepts grammar-valid tokens, so *unconstrained* draft\nmodels waste most of their draft budget on tokens the FSM will reject:\n\n- Measured with vLLM 0.11.2 + xgrammar + Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (A100, batch=1,\n  agentic JSON workload, K=5): unmasked EAGLE3 accepted **1.44** draft tokens\n  per propose call and was **0.97× slower than no speculation**.\n- In an earlier n-gram draft experiment on the same workload, **84.9%** of\n  drafted tokens were rejected by the grammar FSM (292/344).\n\nThis matches the open roadmap item\n*\"Improve configuration ergonomics and robustness with tool calling,\nreasoning, and structured outputs\"* (SIG Spec Decode, Q4 2026 roadmap\n#61024).\n\n## Proposal: grammar-masked draft logits\n\nApply the grammar bitmask **inside the draft loop**, after `compute_logits`\nand before `argmax`, at every speculative position — not only at final\nverification:\n\n1. `Eagle3LlamaForCausalLM.compute_logits` already scatters reduced\n   draft-vocab logits into a **target-vocab-sized** tensor via `d2t`\n   (with `-inf` elsewhere), so the standard xgrammar bitmask\n   (`apply_token_bitmask_inplace`) applies directly with no vocab mapping.\n2. Maintain one `xgrammar.GrammarMatcher` per request. At propose position\n   `t >= 1`, advance the matcher with the token drafted at `t-1`\n   (`self.input_ids[:batch]` in `EagleProposer.propose`); fill the bitmask\n   for each active request row; mask; then `argmax`.\n3. Do **not** rely on `GrammarMatcher.rollback()` for the\n   drafted-but-rejected window: in our measurements it desyncs after a\n   permanent accepted prefix when the window contains forced-continuation\n   tokens (see observation 2). Instead, build a **fresh matcher per propose\n   call**, replay the committed token history (`sampled_token_ids` from\n   `prepare_next_token_ids_*`), mask, then discard the matcher at propose\n   exit. Measured cost is acceptable; FSM replay rejections 0/2387.\n4. If the EAGLE3 draft-vocab subset contains no grammar-valid token for a\n   row, fall back to the first grammar-valid token (worst case: one wasted\n   draft slot; validity unaffected — measured fallback rate: 0/4486 masks).\n\nMeasured effect (same hardware/workload as above; single-session\nmeasurement — 12 distinct agentic prompts, 3 timing iterations, rotated\nconfig order, diagnostics disabled in the hot path):\n\n| Config | TPOT (ms) | tok/propose | vs no_spec | vs baseline |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| no_spec | 11.09 ± 0.01 | — | 1.00× | — |\n| EAGLE3 (unmasked) | 11.47 ± 0.04 | 1.443 | 0.967× | 1.00× |\n| **EAGLE3 + grammar mask** | **9.61 ± 0.03** | **1.969 (+36.5%)** | **1.154×** | **1.193×** |\n\nAcceptance rises because **39.1%** of draft-position argmaxes change under\nthe mask (1756/4486 positions) — the mask removes grammar-invalid\nhigh-probability drafts so the next-most-likely *valid* token is drafted\ninstead. The advantage holds at every draft length tested: K=3 1.15×,\nK=5 1.19×, K=7 1.17× (best absolute: K=3 grammar, 1.25× vs no_spec).\n\nOn a second, enum-heavy tool-call schema (nested enums + arrays):\n\n| Config | Valid JSON | TPOT (ms) | tok/propose |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| no_spec | 5/8 | 11.02 | — |\n| EAGLE3 (unmasked) | **3/8** | 13.54 | 1.203 |\n| **EAGLE3 + grammar mask** | **5/8** | **10.37 (1.31× vs baseline)** | **1.807 (+50.2%)** |\n\nTwo further observations relevant to this roadmap item:\n\n1. **Constrained spec decode currently degrades validity** on hard schemas\n   (3/8 vs 5/8 no_spec — the unmasked drafts push decoding into\n   degeneration loops that hit the token cap). Grammar-masking the drafts\n   *restores* no_spec validity (5/8) while also being faster.\n2. **Avoid `GrammarMatcher.rollback()` for this use case (xgrammar 0.1.25)**:\n   in our measurements it desyncs after a permanent accepted prefix when the\n   rolled-back window contains forced-continuation tokens (e.g. char-splatted\n   enum values) — the matcher returns without error but stops reflecting the\n   true grammar state (minimal repro available on request). A correct\n   implementation should snapshot/restore matcher state or create a fresh\n   matcher per propose and replay committed history (the prototype's v4 mode;\n   FSM replay rejections 0/2387, zero rollback calls).\n\n## Where this would live\n\n`vllm/v1/spec_decode/eagle.py` — `EagleProposer.propose()` currently calls\n`self.model.compute_logits(...)` then `argmax`. A `ConstrainedProposer`\nwrapper (or a bitmask hook on the proposer) could mirror how the target-side\nguided-sampling path integrates xgrammar, reusing the existing per-engine\n`XGrammarBackend`/`TokenizerInfo`.\n\nA working prototype (monkey-patched `EagleProposer`, flag-gated, fresh\nmatcher per propose + committed-history replay, zero rollback calls) is\nbenchmarked here: https://github.com/YuvrajSinghBhadoria2/grammar-spec-decode\n\n## Known limitations (prototype)\n\n- xgrammar matcher runs on CPU: one small host sync per draft position;\n  a GPU-resident FSM would remove it.\n- JSON grammars permit degeneration loops (e.g., endless legal whitespace\n  tokens) — a general constrained-decoding issue, not spec-decode-specific.\n- Evaluated at batch=1 only; batched propose + bitmask indices path\n  (`apply_token_bitmask_inplace(..., indices=...)`) is ready but untested.\n- One model/head pair (Qwen2.5-7B + community EAGLE3 head) and two JSON\n  schemas so far.\n\n## Repro\n\nPrototype + benchmarks (vLLM 0.11.2, xgrammar 0.1.25):\nhttps://github.com/YuvrajSinghBhadoria2/grammar-spec-decode","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62130/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62130/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/51986","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/51986/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/51986/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/51986/events","html_url":"https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/51986","id":5130537029,"node_id":"I_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABMc3IRQ","number":51986,"title":"[Bug]: mnnvl allreduce workspace init hangs 30s and leaks GPU memory on IB-only multi-node","user":{"login":"appleparan","id":156580,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE1NjU4MA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/156580?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/appleparan","html_url":"https://github.com/appleparan","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/appleparan/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/appleparan/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/appleparan/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/appleparan/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/appleparan/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/appleparan/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/appleparan/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/appleparan/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/appleparan/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":5135047172,"node_id":"LA_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABMhKaBA","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/labels/bug","name":"bug","color":"d73a4a","default":true,"description":"Something isn't working","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":11639749480,"node_id":"LA_kwDOI7xefs8AAAACtch7aA","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/labels/kimi","name":"kimi","color":"78d0bd","default":false,"description":null,"archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":7,"created_at":"2026-08-12T11:49:24Z","updated_at":"2026-10-18T16:49:05Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Your current environment\n\n<details>\n<summary>The output of <code>python collect_env.py</code></summar