\n[pip3] nvidia-nvshmem-cu13==3.4.5\n[pip3] nvidia-nvtx==13.2.75\n[pip3] nvidia-nvvm==13.4.92\n[pip3] pyzmq==27.2.0\n[pip3] tokenspeed-triton==3.9.10.post20261008\n[pip3] torch==2.14.0+cu132\n[pip3] torch_c_dlpack_ext==0.1.5\n[pip3] torchaudio==2.11.0+cpu\n[pip3] torchcodec==0.17.0+cu132\n[pip3] torchvision==0.29.0+cu132\n[pip3] transformers==5.19.0\n[pip3] triton==3.8.0\n[conda] No relevant packages\n==============================\n         vLLM Info\n==============================\nROCM Version                 : Could not collect\nvLLM Version                 : 0.32.0rc1.dev342+gf98ae6a9f (git sha: f98ae6a9f)\nvLLM Build Flags:\n  CUDA Archs: Not Set; ROCm: Disabled; XPU: Disabled\nGPU Topology:\n  \t\u001B[4mGPU0\tCPU Affinity\tNUMA Affinity\tGPU NUMA ID\u001B[0m\nGPU0\t X \t0-19\t0\t\tN/A\nLegend:\n  X    = Self\n  SYS  = Connection traversing PCIe as well as the SMP interconnect between NUMA nodes (e.g., QPI/UPI)\n  NODE = Connection traversing PCIe as well as the interconnect between PCIe Host Bridges within a NUMA node\n  PHB  = Connection traversing PCIe as well as a PCIe Host Bridge (typically the CPU)\n  PXB  = Connection traversing multiple PCIe bridges (without traversing the PCIe Host Bridge)\n  PIX  = Connection traversing at most a single PCIe bridge\n  NV#  = Connection traversing a bonded set of # NVLinks\n==============================\n     Environment Variables\n==============================\nPYTORCH_NVML_BASED_CUDA_CHECK=1\nTORCHINDUCTOR_COMPILE_THREADS=1\nTORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR=/tmp/torchinductor_spark-dvs\n```\n</details>\n\nNVIDIA DGX Spark (GB10, sm_121, aarch64), driver 580.173.02, CUDA 13.0, torch 2.14.0+cu132, vLLM main @ f98ae6a9f, flashinfer-python 0.7.2 (provides the `b12x` module).\n\n### 🐛 Describe the bug\n\n`--attention-backend b12x` without `--block-size` fails at engine start, on both the uniproc and multiproc executors:\n\n```\nValueError: b12x requires --block-size in (64, 128), got 16.\n```\n\nRepro, which is the command from `docs/design/attention_backends.md`:\n\n```bash\nvllm serve Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B --attention-backend b12x\n```\n\nor offline:\n\n```python\nfrom vllm import LLM\nLLM(\"Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B\", attention_config={\"backend\": \"B12X\"})\n```\n\nCause: `B12xPagedAttentionImpl.__init__` checks `cache_config.block_size` against `(64, 128)`. The impl is built during `load_model()`, but `Platform.update_block_size_for_backend()`, which would replace the default 16 with the backend's preferred 128, only runs after `load_model()` (it needs the model's attention layers to find the backends). So the check always sees 16 unless the user sets `--block-size` explicitly.\n\nThe check is also redundant: a user-specified unsupported size is already rejected at backend selection via `supports_block_size()`, and the runtime page size comes from the KV cache tensors. The existing tests in `tests/v1/attention/test_b12x.py` pass `block_size=64/128` explicitly, so they never hit this.\n\nWorkaround: pass `--block-size 64` or `--block-size 128`.\n\nI have a fix with a regression test; PR follows.\n\n### Before submitting a new issue...\n\n- [x] Make sure you already searched for relevant issues, and asked the chatbot living at the bottom right corner of the [documentation page](https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/), which can answer lots of frequently asked questions.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62264/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/62264/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61423","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61423/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61423/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61423/events","html_url":"https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61423","id":5831604041,"node_id":"I_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABW5c3SQ","number":61423,"title":"[RFC]: Re-evaluate per-iteration metrics as a foundation for orchestrator observability","user":{"login":"markmc","id":15288,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE1Mjg4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/15288?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/markmc","html_url":"https://github.com/markmc","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/markmc/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/markmc/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/markmc/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/markmc/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/markmc/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/markmc/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/markmc/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/markmc/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/markmc/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":6478559908,"node_id":"LA_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABgib6pA","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/labels/RFC","name":"RFC","color":"DE9220","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":3,"created_at":"2026-10-13T13:54:31Z","updated_at":"2026-10-18T06:55:30Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"MEMBER","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"## Motivation\n\nOrchestrators such as NVIDIA Dynamo need consistent observations of scheduled work and execution time, as described in [#38760](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/38760) and [#52061](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/52061). Before extending this telemetry, I propose cleaning up the semantics and collection of our existing per-iteration statistics.\n\nIteration logging was introduced in [#31193](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/31193) and moved to the frontend in [#46647](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/46647). Several inconsistencies now deserve attention:\n\n- Console batch statistics and Prometheus observations use different accounting boundaries.\n- Existing iteration logging infers prefill/decode phase from worker-cache state and output history. After output has begun, model runner V1 counts all recomputation chunks as decode; V2 counts only the first chunk as prefill.\n- `elapsed_ms` measures EngineCore's remaining result wait, which can be highly misleading with queued execution.\n- Frontend `IterationStats` describes request-output processing, creating ambiguity around what an \"iteration\" means.\n\nThe objective is a shared, inexpensive collection foundation that supports console logging, Prometheus, and subsequent orchestrator integrations.\n\n## Proposed change\n\n### Define the scheduled batch contract\n\nAn **iteration** describes the execution of **the target model's scheduled batch**. Internal microbatching and speculative draft-model invocations do not create additional iteration records.\n\nUse the scheduler's view of each request's execution phase:\n\n| Statistic | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `num_prefill_requests` | Requests scheduled in prefill during this iteration. |\n| `num_decode_requests` | Requests scheduled in decode during this iteration. |\n| `num_prefill_tokens` | Input token positions assigned to those prefill requests. |\n| `num_decode_tokens` | Input token positions assigned to those decode requests. |\n\nThese categories describe execution phase, independently of a worker's configured prefill/decode role.\n\n- Every recomputation chunk counts as prefill, including rebuilding previously generated context.\n- Processing newly streamed input re-enters prefill.\n- The first scheduled execution after remote-prefill admission on a decode worker counts as prefill, including any rewind.\n- The final prefill execution remains prefill; subsequent generation is decode, including when preceding outputs are still pending under async scheduling.\n\nFreeze counts at scheduling time. Include scheduled recomputation, replay, and speculative positions; exclude reused KV positions and additional worker padding. Later rejection, aborts, or output truncation do not revise them. Draft-model executions have no separate contribution to these four counts.\n\n### Share collection and observation\n\nAccumulate counts during existing scheduler passes, avoiding another traversal of scheduled requests. Carry the snapshot through the existing stats route to one frontend per engine.\n\nReuse engine/DP-rank-local iteration identity. Explicitly mark dummy execution, empty scheduling steps, and heartbeats so diagnostic consumers can retain them while request-work histograms exclude them.\n\nFollowing [#53550](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/53550), observe the four quantities as histograms. Each eligible batch contributes observations for both phases, including zero when a phase is absent. Include intermediate prefill batches that produce no request outputs.\n\nCorrect `vllm:iteration_tokens_total` to observe scheduled input tokens once per eligible batch, following and crediting [#51261](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/51261). This fix can use the existing scheduler total even if phase collection remains opt-in.\n\nClarify frontend `IterationStats` as request-output accounting, potentially renaming it `RequestOutputStats`. Preserve request-level prompt, returned-token, and lifecycle semantics.\n\n### Replace the execution-time measurement\n\nIn a subsequent change, replace the measurement behind `elapsed_ms` with a worker CUDA-stream interval: immediately before target-model execution through sampling and speculative drafting for the next batch.\n\nThis interval includes CPU submission delays that leave the stream idle inside its boundaries, and excludes GPU work queued before the start event. It describes scheduled-batch execution rather than a sum of kernel durations.\n\nCollect completed CUDA-event timings asynchronously without blocking GPU synchronization or delaying request outputs. Associate timings with the original scheduling snapshot and deliver complete records in later `EngineCoreOutputs`, including stats-only packets when necessary. Observe each record once.\n\n### Benchmark before choosing defaults\n\nThe preferred outcome is collection with `log_stats`, independently of verbose iteration logging. Keep new collection opt-in until benchmarks establish acceptable overhead; evaluate collection, histogram observation, and worker timing separately.\n\nStart with short-context decode on a small model using two A100 80GB GPUs, sweeping batch sizes and async scheduling. Compare main, the patch with collection disabled, and the patch with collection enabled while console output is suppressed. Include representative larger-model serving workloads and report measurement uncertainty.\n\nCounts may become routine operational telemetry while execution timing remains opt-in.\n\n### Implement in small changes\n\n1. Efficient scheduler collection and precise phase/token semantics, retaining the existing iteration-logging option and consumer names temporarily where useful.\n2. Phase-aware Prometheus histograms.\n3. The aggregate token-histogram correction, independently of phase-count enablement.\n4. Worker execution timing, asynchronous record delivery, and the duration histogram.\n\nFrontend naming cleanup can proceed independently.\n\n## Follow-on work\n\nContext-length statistics, scheduler queue statistics, and low-latency orchestrator reporting build on this foundation. Their populations, observation boundaries, freshness requirements, and export contracts need separate designs.\n\nThis work also supports future performance-model calibration such as [#55259](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/55259), and complements the broader observability discussion in [#39979](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/39979).\n\nFeedback is particularly welcome on the phase definitions, execution-time scope, benchmark coverage, and implementation sequence.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61423/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/61423/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/59897","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/59897/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/59897/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/issues/59897/events","html_url":"https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/59897","id":5693445517,"node_id":"I_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABU1sVjQ","number":59897,"title":"[Usage]: How to serve qwen3.8-flash-next-fp8 on 2 A100","user":{"login":"Alireza3242","id":77293766,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc3MjkzNzY2","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/77293766?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Alireza3242","html_url":"https://github.com/Alireza3242","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Alireza3242/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Alireza3242/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Alireza3242/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Alireza3242/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Alireza3242/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Alireza3242/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Alireza3242/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Alireza3242/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Alireza3242/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":6697501599,"node_id":"LA_kwDOI7xefs8AAAABjzPDnw","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/labels/usage","name":"usage","color":"fef2c0","default":false,"description":"How to use vllm","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":1,"created_at":"2026-10-03T20:54:30Z","updated_at":"2026-10-18T06:52:01Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Your current environment\n\n```text\nCollecting environment information...\n==============================\n        System Info\n==============================\nOS                           : Ubuntu 24.04.3 LTS (x86_64)\nGCC version                  : (Ubuntu 13.3.0-6ubuntu2~24.04.1) 13.3.0\nClang version                : Could not collect\nCMake version                : Could not collect\nLibc version                 : glibc-2.39\n\n==============================\n       PyTorch Info\n==============================\nPyTorch version              : 2.13.0+cu130\nIs debug build               : False\nCUDA used to build PyTorch   : 13.0\nROCM used to build PyTorch   : N/A\nXPU used to build PyTorch    : N/A\n\n==============================\n      Python Environment\n===============